// The Hive Tour — an HTTP server that walks through the language one
// feature at a time, in the spirit of an interactive language tour. Every
// slide is one `slides.Slide` value: a title, an explanation, and one or
// more code examples — each a complete, runnable program in its own right,
// kept small enough that one example makes one point. Every piece of prose
// carries an English and a Portuguese version side by side; the code never
// does, because code is not prose. `site.handle` reads the slide number and
// the chosen language out of the URL, `hive.net.httpServe` does the rest.
//
// This file starts the server, and is the only one of the program's files at
// the top of the project. The server itself — every page, every route, and
// what the head of each page says — is ./site, a module of its own so that the
// tests, under tests/ and each a program of its own, can import it: a
// `with compiler` call may only be written in the file handed to `hive build`,
// so the one in `main` below sits in a file no test imports. A comment here
// that names a module as ./theme means src/theme.hive; a comment in src/ means
// the file beside it, as its import does. The other files under src/ hold the
// things that are not the server, each because a program is easier to read
// when the thing it does and the material it does it to are not interleaved:
//
//   src/slides    the tour itself, seventy-five slides of it in two languages,
//                  and the two types that hold one. Four fifths of the whole
//                  program by weight, and none of it about HTTP.
//   src/home       what the front page says: the case for the language, made to
//                  somebody deciding whether to use it, in the same two
//                  languages and with samples held to the same rule.
//   src/logo       the mark, as the paths of an SVG. A drawing is not prose,
//                  and a page of path coordinates in the middle of some would
//                  read as neither.
//   src/icon       the same mark as pixels, in the three raster shapes a
//                  search engine, a browser, and an iOS home screen or a
//                  pasted link respectively want. It exists because neither
//                  Google Search nor any link unfurler will read the SVG the
//                  other two files are perfectly happy with.
//   src/theme      how it all looks: the one stylesheet this program writes,
//                  layered over the one `ui.page` writes for itself.
//   src/fonts      the three typefaces it is set in, carried in the binary so
//                  nothing on a page is fetched from anywhere but here.
//   src/rail       railroad diagrams — a grammar rule drawn as track — drawn
//                  with `hive.ui`'s own canvas, and the line of the tour
//                  drawn in the same notation across the top of a slide.
//   src/chart      the symbol on each slide's tile, for the chart the contents
//                  page is and the front page ends on.
//   src/grammar    the rules of Hive's grammar it draws, copied from the spec,
//                  and which slide teaches which.
//   src/highlight  a Hive lexer, for painting the samples in ./slides. It is
//                  here rather than in a script tag because the home page
//                  claims there is no framework underneath this site, and that
//                  claim is worth more than the eight lines it saves.
//   src/play       the playground: which samples can run in a reader's
//                  browser, what to say about one that cannot, and the one
//                  script this site ships, which runs them there. It says
//                  why there is a script at all.
//   src/compile.go the build behind the playground's Run button — the one
//                  file not written in Hive, because it has to be able to give
//                  up on a build, and Hive cannot stop a program it started.
//   src/blog       the blog's posts: which there are, and each one read out of
//                  the Markdown files under assets/blog/ that the build puts
//                  inside the executable.
//   src/markdown   the Markdown those posts are written in, turned into HTML.
//   src/news       every release of the language, its changelog, and who it
//                  has to thank, all of it asked of GitHub while the site
//                  builds rather than while it runs.
import ./src/news
import ./src/site

// `httpServe` binds `:8080` — every interface, not just the loopback — which
// is what makes this the same program in a container as on a laptop.
//
// The line it prints says the port it bound and *then* the URL, in that order,
// and the order is the whole point of it. It used to print the URL alone, and
// inside a container that is a sentence which is true for the program and
// false for the person reading it: the server really is answering on
// localhost:8080, on the container's localhost, which is not the one the
// browser is pointed at. A container started without `-p 8080:8080` then looks
// exactly like a working one — it builds, it runs, it says it is serving, and
// nothing loads — and the log is the reason the search starts in the wrong
// place. Naming the bound port first says what was actually done; the URL
// after it is the convenience it always was, for the case where the machine
// running this is the machine holding the browser.
//
// The releases on the News page are the one thing here the build fetches:
// `with compiler` runs `news.fetch` once, on the machine that builds the site,
// and what it answered is built into the executable in the call's place. So
// a running server never asks GitHub for anything, and a release is on the
// page the next time the site is built. ./news says what is asked, and why a
// build that cannot ask fails.
proc main(): void {
	releases := news.fetch() with compiler
	echo "The Hive Tour — listening on :8080 (locally, http://localhost:8080)"
	hive.net.httpServe(8080, site.handle(_, releases))
}
